course: biology
lesson: 143
title: Bambi: real deer, rabbits, skunks and owls
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Bambi, a spotted fawn wobbles on thin legs, Thumper the rabbit thumps his foot, Flower the skunk blushes and Friend Owl spins his head all the way round.
explain: Much of this is truer than you might think: real fawns are spotted and stand within about an hour of birth, and real rabbits really do thump to warn each other.
explain: Some of it is stretched: an owl turns its head about three-quarters of the way round, not a full circle, and deer avoid frozen ponds because hard hooves slip on smooth ice.
explain: Imagine walking through long grass and finding a spotted fawn lying perfectly still. It is not lost: its mother hid it there on purpose and is feeding nearby, so the kind thing is to leave it and walk away.
fact: Fawns have white spots on a reddish-brown coat that look like sunlight on the forest floor, and they can stand within about an hour of birth.
fact: A mother deer hides her fawn for hours while she feeds nearby; the fawn has very little smell and stays still, and a fawn found alone should usually be left.
fact: Antlers are bone and grow new every year; only male deer grow them, except reindeer, where females do too, and growing antlers are covered in soft velvet.
fact: White-tailed deer raise their white tails as they bound away, warning others and helping fawns follow; the book's Bambi was a European roe deer, the film's a white-tailed deer.
fact: Rabbits thump their back feet to warn others of danger, and the thump travels through the ground into burrows.
fact: Real skunks are fairly calm and spray only as a last resort, after stamping, hissing and raising the tail; refilling the spray takes days.
fact: A skunk's bold black and white pattern makes predators notice and remember it; great horned owls, with a poor sense of smell, are among the few that eat skunks.
fact: Owls are mostly active at night and can turn their heads about 270 degrees, three-quarters of the way round, not a full circle.
fact: Owl eyes are fixed like tubes, so owls turn their heads instead, using 14 neck bones, twice as many as our 7.
fact: Deer often freeze in bright headlights: the sudden light dazzles eyes made for the dark, and freezing is their first answer to danger.
fact: Forest fires drive animals out, but many forests recover; some pine cones open only in fire's heat, and deer feed on the fresh growth afterwards.
fact: Hard deer hooves grip soil and snow but slip on smooth ice, so real deer avoid frozen ponds where they can.
exercise: What is a real fawn's coat like? | Reddish-brown with white spots | Plain grey with no spots | Black with white stripes | Golden with brown rings
exercise: How soon after birth can a fawn stand? | Within about an hour | Only after about a month | Only after about a year | Only after it can walk on ice
exercise: You find a fawn lying alone and still in long grass. What is usually right? | Leave it and walk away; its mother is nearby | Pick it up gently and take it home to keep it safe and warm | Give it milk from the fridge | Stay beside it until night
exercise: What are deer antlers made of? | Bone, grown new every year | Hair, pressed very tightly together | Horn, like a cow's, kept for life | Teeth that grow out of the head
exercise: Which deer grow antlers? | Males, and in reindeer females too | Only the females of every kind of deer | Every deer, from the day it is born | Only the oldest deer in the whole herd
exercise: Why does a white-tailed deer flash its white tail as it runs? | To warn others and help fawns follow | To scare the wolf with a bright light | To cool its body down while it runs fast | To show the others it is the fastest deer
exercise: Why does a rabbit like Thumper thump its back foot? | To warn other rabbits of danger | To dig a hole in the ground | To call its mother to dinner | To shake mud off its paw
exercise: What does a real skunk do before it sprays? | Stamps, hisses and raises its tail as a warning | Sprays at once without any warning | Rolls into a spiky ball first | Climbs the nearest tree and sprays down on the animal from above
exercise: Why are a skunk's black and white stripes so bold? | So predators notice them and remember to stay away | So the skunk can hide in the snow | So other skunks can find each other easily on bright sunny days | So the skunk looks like a zebra
exercise: How far round can an owl turn its head? | About three-quarters of the way round | A full circle and more | Only a little to each side | Exactly halfway, no further
exercise: Why does an owl turn its head instead of its eyes? | Its eyes are fixed in place like tubes | It has no muscles at all in its round face | It is checking which way the wind blows | Its eyes are on the back of its head too
exercise: Why do deer often freeze in a car's headlights? | The light dazzles eyes made for the dark | They want to see who is driving | They like the warm light | They are waiting for the car to stop
exercise: What happens to a forest after a fire? | Many forests recover, and some plants need fire | Nothing ever grows there again, and the ground stays black for good | Only weeds grow there forever | The animals never come back
exercise: Why do real deer avoid a frozen pond? | Their hard hooves slip on smooth ice | They are afraid of the fish below | Ice burns their hooves | They cannot see the ice at all
exercise: How many neck bones does an owl have? | 14 | 7 | 3 | 70
test: «saw 3 fawns and their 2 mums in the field, how many legs is that» | 20 | 12 | 5 | 16
test: «Owl on telly turned his head right round, full circle, owls can do that innit» What is right? | No, about 270 degrees, three-quarters of the way | Yes, owls can spin their heads all the way round, like a wheel on an axle | Yes, two full turns if they want | No, owls cannot turn their heads at all
test: «found a baby deer on its own, poor thing's been dumped, I'm taking it home» What is right? | Leave it; its mother hid it there on purpose and will be back | Take it home, wrap it in a blanket and keep it warm until it is grown | Feed it bread and leave it | Chase it so it finds its mother
test: In a cartoon, Bambi's mum has big antlers too. What is real for white-tailed deer? | Only males grow antlers; among deer, only reindeer females grow them | Females grow the biggest antlers | Both males and females grow antlers, and they stay on their heads for life | Neither grows antlers; they are horns
test: «skunks spray at anything for fun, they're evil» What is right? | They spray only as a last resort, after warnings | Yes, they spray every animal they meet, the moment they see it coming | Yes, they spray just for fun whenever they are bored | No, skunks cannot spray at all; that is only in cartoons
test: What would happen if an owl's neck bent only as far as yours? | It would see less around it, because its eyes cannot move | It would see much better | It would fly much faster, because big eyes make the head heavy in the air | It would hear less well
test: «crap, a deer just stood there in my lights and wouldn't move, thick animal» Why did it freeze? | The bright light dazzled it, and freezing is its first answer to danger | Deer are too stupid to understand that a car is coming, so they just stand there | It wanted to fight the car | It was asleep standing up
test: Why might a great horned owl eat a skunk when foxes leave it alone? | The owl has a poor sense of smell, so the spray bothers it less | Owls cannot see black and white | Skunks cannot lift their tails high enough to spray upwards at a bird in the air | Owls eat only animals that smell bad
test: «why's that rabbit drumming its foot, is it angry with me» What is it most likely doing? | Warning other rabbits that danger is near | Dancing to music | Saying hello to you | Asking for a carrot
test: In Bambi the fire destroys the forest forever. What is real? | Many forests recover, and deer feed on the fresh plants after | Forests never grow back after a fire | Deer cannot live anywhere there has been a fire, so they leave that forest for good | Fire turns the soil into stone
test: Bambi slides on the ice with legs going everywhere. Is that true to life? | Yes, hard hooves slip on smooth ice, so deer avoid it | No, deer hooves stick to ice like glue, so they can walk across frozen ponds safely | No, deer skate well on frozen ponds | Yes, but only fawns slip, never adults
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a fawn discovered alone in long grass. | The fawn has customarily been concealed by its mother, who is feeding nearby, and should be left undisturbed | The fawn has evidently been abandoned by its mother and requires prompt removal to a dwelling for its care | The fawn requires immediate feeding with cow's milk | The fawn ought to be pursued so as to rejoin its mother
exam: Identify the maximum rotation of the head of which an owl is capable. | Approximately two hundred and seventy degrees | A complete revolution of three hundred and sixty degrees | Two complete revolutions | Approximately ninety degrees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the defensive conduct of the skunk? | The animal discharges its spray only as a last resort, after warning displays | The animal discharges its spray at all creatures it encounters, without any preliminary warning whatsoever | The animal discharges its spray for amusement | The animal is incapable of any discharge
exam: Ascertain the composition and renewal of the antlers of deer. | They consist of bone and are shed and regrown annually | They consist of hair and are retained for life | They consist of horn, like those of cattle, and grow without cessation throughout the life of the animal | They consist of teeth projecting from the skull
